pmdomain: mediatek: mt6797: power the MFG shader-core domains (split SRAM ACK)

The follow-up pmdomain/0003 and 0004 deferred: MFG_COREx has its SRAM PDN
bit in one register and the matching ACK in another, which neither
ext_sram_ctl_offs nor the generic single-register SRAM engine can
express.

  MFG        PDN and ACK both in MFG_SRAM_CON 0x33C   -> ext_sram_ctl_offs
  MFG_COREn  PDN is bit 8 of MFG_COREn_PWR_CON,
             ACK is bit 20+n of MFG_SRAM_CON          -> ext_sram_ack_offs

(vendor clk-mt6797-pg.c, spm_mtcmos_ctrl_mfg_core0..3: "Set SRAM_PDN = 0"
writes MFG_CORE0_PWR_CON, then "Wait until MFG_CORE0_SRAM_PDN_ACK = 0"
polls MFG_SRAM_CON.)

So split the one address the SRAM helpers took into two — where the PDN
bit is written, and where its ACK is read — and add ext_sram_ack_offs for
the second. Every existing domain passes the same address for both and is
unaffected.

Measured on hardware before this landed, with panfrost bound and
runtime-resumed: PWR_STATUS 0x2B00305E (MFG and MFG_ASYNC on, cores bits
8..11 off), MFG_COREn_PWR_CON all 0x112 — untouched at reset — and on the
GPU side L2_READY 0x1, SHADER_READY 0x0, SHADER_PWRTRANS 0xF: all four
cores stuck mid-transition because nothing had ungated them. That is what
"error powering up gpu shader" was.

Measured after, same conditions: PWR_STATUS 0x2B003F5E (bits 8..13 all
set), MFG_SRAM_CON 0x000000FC (every SRAM ACK clear), SHADER_READY 0xF,
TILER_READY 0x1, SHADER_PWRTRANS 0x0. The shader cores power up.

Signed-off-by: mratan <mattdratan@gmail.com>

